nd learners who are native speakers of  German or of Spanish or Chinese and  they'll quote you you know proverbs  translated from their own languages in  order to explain something in English  and they think that's very good because  they like their own thing and it's  normal you you you have a certain  attachment to it's an emotional  attachment either to your own language  or say in the case of my speaking  Spanish and Portuguese I'm quite  comfortable with my Spanish  pronunciation and so to start  pronouncing essentially the same words  in quite a different way is something  that I'm at some level reluctant to do  my emotions are getting in the way  whereas if it's a totally different  language I just go for it 100% it's it's  totally make-believe imaginary I'm  playing some strange role and and so I'm  not held back so I think this can be the  case with very similar languages like if  we I speak English I could live in in  the United in Texas or Scotland for 20  years I wouldn't change how I speak I  might be slightly influenced by it but  not very much I'm comfortable with it  I'm not gonna change it whereas a  foreigner moving into those any of those  areas with it would probably be more  influenced by the local language more  trying harder wanting to imitate the  local pronunciation so I just mentioned  this because a an answer to the video  from Lucas I think that he has to try to  project himself as an Argentinian and  it's easier for him and perhaps more  attractive for him to project himself as  an Italian or an American let's say  that's fun that's positive for him he  likes doing that but yeah he doesn't  want to project themselves in  Argentinian because the Spanish are  whatever he whoever he speaks to  Paraguayan or whoever neighbors of  Brazil because  got some lovely things they should be  able to understand his Portuguese ease  he's not as motivated and I see this in  so many different areas I see this for  example where people from certain  cultures like to consider themselves  very unique and they're further  unwilling to let go of that cultural  identity that cultural ego in order to  project themselves into this other  cultural identity as I say my father you  see people even from the same language  group let's say French people and some  will pronounce English very very well  and others make no concession at all to  to English as they speak English like  this and that is the way they will only  speak it because they at some emotional  level they don't want to change so uh  you know I just want to get reaction  from people honest I think in so many  things in language learning our  willingness our emotional commitment to  our resistance to certain things our  determination our ability to project  into another persona these things are so  important so important far more  important than diagrams of the mouth and  where you put your tongue or or any  other of these artificial learning you  know tricks or tools or assists that  that you can find so I guess the the  message there is to really look at your  at your attitude and your emotional  commitment to learning a language and to  pronouncing that language you know  closer to the way it's pronounced so I  mean I I'd like to hear from Lukas I  think that's his problem I think if you  talk about learning a language what does  it mean to learn a language a Portuguese  person practically doesn't have to learn  Spanish they know 95% of the vocabulary  and the grammar is so similar that  yeah there's differences but they're not  great I mean they're basically there so  there's no reason why and I don't  believe those who say well you know  because the pronunciation is so  different you know Portuguese which has  all these diphthongs and and Spanish  which has they're very clear pure vowels  that it's more difficult for that reason  I don't think it's difficult for those  sort of technical reasons I think it's  difficult because and the proof of that  is if you listen to someone like like  Benny the Irish polyglot or Luka or  Richard simcard they pronounce each of  the Romance languages very well they  speak Portuguese very well Spanish very  well Italian very well French very well  so they are able to make all the  different sounds so the only reason a  native speaker of Portuguese Brazilian  Portuguese like Lucas is not is because  he doesn't want to I haven't heard him  an Italian but he pronounces English  very well  so it comes down to this attitudinal  thing so you know I think we can take a  lesson from that and look at how that  applies to our own language learning are  we resisting at some level are we  hanging back are we reluctant yeah  attitude is big so I look forward to  hearing your comments and maybe even  hearing from Lucas on that bye for now
 

